WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.580
 From the 1960s to the 1980s, the U.S. Department of Defense was investing in a research network

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:12.840
 called ARPANET, and ARPANET was funded by the military ARPA Defense Advanced Research

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:19.660
 Projects Agency, and we'll meet, later in the lecture, we'll meet, Vint Cerf will tell

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:24.540
 the story much better than I can tell, but I'll just tell you a short version right now.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:29.800
 A lot of people wonder why the Department of Defense started this project, and a lot

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:35.260
 of people say, "Oh, it's because of the fear of nuclear war," and the project was

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:40.660
 very long, and there were various motivations throughout the various phases of the effort,

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:45.960
 but based on what I've been told and what I've read, I think it's safe to say that

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:51.940
 the primary motivation was to improve the use of their computing equipment that they

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:56.280
 were used for military purposes, make it easier to access them, make it so people didn't

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:00.780
 have to travel as much, make it so that people could work from their office on many different

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:06.540
 computers, and also so that people could actually talk more effectively, so they could send

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:10.620
 email real fast, so that the email would move faster for military people.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:15.880
 So the first thing is really kind of an end user focus to make them more useful to people

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.180
 and get people working together more effectively.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:21.640
 That was the first kind of heavy motivation.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:29.700
 The second motivation actually had to do with reliability and redundancy and resistance

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:35.200
 to partial failure, and that really wasn't so much about nuclear attack.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.760
 That was more about battlefield attack, so the notion that once you start having all

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:43.500
 these links and you make them wireless and you put them in trailers on a battlefield

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:49.140
 and you got a few hundred miles and the data's moving back and forth, then we have to worry

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:53.260
 about in a battlefield situation, one of those trailers might get hit and how would you keep

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:55.380
 the network running if one of those trailers got hit.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:02:02.300
 So the notion that we have sort of a whole nation and we are worried about communications,

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:06.340
 I'm sure there was some worry about that, but it wasn't really the main reason.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:11.780
 I mean, the exciting thing to me is the main reason was a very people-centered reason of

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:15.320
 people working more effectively.

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:22.020
 But this was a rather exclusive club, rather exclusive network, because it was only for

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:26.200
 the people who were either military or funded to build it.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:30.160
 So in 1969, there were four hosts on it.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:31.820
 Again, that's research.

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:35.820
 And these were lease lines, the same ones we were all using to do our email, and they

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:38.380
 were expensive, very expensive.

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:42.740
 But because it was a large grant, they just paid it because the research was not about

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:43.740
 the money.

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:47.340
 The research was, if you had these connections, what would be the best way to use them, and

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.900
 how to deal with redundancies, and how to deal with multiple paths to the same place.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:58.700
 All that stuff is interesting research questions that computer scientists spent many years

00:02:58.700 --> 00:02:59.700
 conceiving of.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:02.940
 And so by 1972, looks like we got about 20.

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:07.820
 We got a couple of cross-country links and multiple connections, and this looks like

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:12.340
 a great test bed to see, you know, so all of a sudden this part goes down, how quickly

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:14.260
 can we reroute around the outage.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:20.220
 And literally these things these days reroute around outages in far less than a second.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:22.600
 And so that's really impressive.

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:29.780
 Our current internet owes its history to the research that was done in the 1960s and 1970s.

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:36.000
 But what was fundamentally different between the store and forward networks of Bitnet and

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:42.460
 this ARPANET research network was the notion of packet switching.

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:44.900
 So it's a real simple concept.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:49.060
 It just takes a little more complex software to solve.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:53.840
 The store and forward network would start sending data across the link, and then keep

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:55.020
 sending it until it was done.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:56.340
 That seemed to be very efficient.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:00.260
 You didn't want to like, you wanted to use it as fast as you can, and then put in the

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:02.340
 next one out, put the next one out, put the next one out.

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:06.740
 It was really simple and it dealt with outages in a simple manner by storing them all locally

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:07.900
 on the disk.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:13.540
 But what if instead you made it so you broke every message, let me see if I can even draw

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:14.540
 this.

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:15.980
 I should probably make a slide about this.

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:21.420
 So if there's a network here and here's my computer and it's got a network connection,

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:28.660
 it's got one really big message broken into pieces, many pieces, and it sends the pieces

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:30.820
 one at a time.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:37.260
 If you show up with a really short message with only three pieces, all you have to do

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:40.460
 is wait for one piece to go and then your piece goes in.

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:43.400
 And then you share for a while, right?

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:48.520
 You share for a while and then you have made it through because you only have three packets.

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:51.160
 This has like 1,000 packets.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:57.180
 And then once your message is through, they resume sending this data in order.

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:02.900
 And so you were able to sneak and bypass the traffic jam that was this large amount of

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:03.900
 data.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:04.900
 And so that's the idea of packets.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:10.060
 And packets are these chunks to say what we're going to send is a piece.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:13.400
 And then at the end of that, we're going to maybe send a piece of a different message.

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:18.620
 It was allowing simultaneously multiple message to be in flight at the same time.

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:20.820
 That's packet switching.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:25.180
 And what happened then is we also ended up with these special purpose computers called

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:26.180
 routers.

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:30.540
 They're still computers, but they weren't doing storing in the same way.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:31.780
 They were just forwarding.

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:32.860
 They weren't storing forward.

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:34.140
 They were merely forwarding.

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:43.980
 So we'll get back to that in a second.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:48.880
 So my favorite analogy to talk about how packets work is postcards.

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:54.140
 So let's say that I want to send a 30-character message to my friend Daphne at Stanford.

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:57.100
 But all I have is 10-character postcards.

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:58.980
 So off I go.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:03.920
 I break my message into three bits of 10 characters.

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:07.060
 I write each piece on a different postcard.

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:12.540
 I address with a from and a to address from Chuck to Daphne.

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:15.820
 And then I give a sequence number so that when she gets the postcards, she knows what

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:18.260
 order to put them back together.

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:19.420
 So I have to label each of these.

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:23.100
 And these are fixed width and fixed length.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:24.700
 It's like a packet.

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:29.020
 I'm breaking a long message that might be very long into nothing so that each piece

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:32.740
 is a certain size so that we can all share it.

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:41.620
 And so then what I do is I send these, I put them all in my mailbox, and then I wait.

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:48.960
 And this magical thing called the post office picks them up, puts them in buildings.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:56.060
 We'll sort them, they end up on trucks, they end up on planes, on trains, on donkeys by

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:57.540
 hand.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:00.580
 And each of my postcards might take a different route.

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:18.660
 I mean, one of my postcards goes from Michigan to Chicago to Kansas City to San Francisco.

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:25.220
 Another of my postcards goes from Michigan to Chicago to Dallas to Denver to Kansas City

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:28.020
 to San Francisco.

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:31.280
 And my third postcard goes really crazy.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:37.460
 It goes to Cleveland, and it goes to Atlanta, then it goes to Washington, DC, and then it

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:44.140
 goes to Chicago, and then it goes to Kansas City, and then it makes it to San Francisco.

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:48.060
 Now the fact that they took different paths and they might not even arrive in order, some

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:52.980
 might get past each other, they might get stuck somewhere, it doesn't matter because

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:57.160
 each one of these has been labeled both with the from and the to destination.

00:07:57.160 --> 00:08:01.180
 So each of these intermediate locations doesn't have to get it to the final destination, they

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:02.820
 just have to get it closer.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:07.580
 They make choices, and even if they make the wrong choice, it can be sort of dealt with

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:09.940
 one way or the other.

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:12.380
 So this is some big mystery.

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:14.040
 It's called the post office.

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:16.420
 It just takes these little cards with labels and moves them.

00:08:16.420 --> 00:08:20.120
 It doesn't know if that's a YouTube video or if it's an email.

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:21.120
 It doesn't really care.

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:24.180
 It just has these little chunks to move around that have from and to addresses.

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:29.380
 I mean, that's the essence of the network, the internet, the cloud.

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:30.980
 That's why we draw this picture.

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:32.380
 You'll see like, "Ooh, it's a cloud."

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:35.420
 And that's because it's, don't worry about what's inside here.

00:08:35.420 --> 00:08:38.840
 It's all super mysterious, and don't worry about it.

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:40.060
 It just comes out.

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:46.800
 So after all that happens, Daphne, who happens to have the exact same mailbox as I do, it's

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:47.800
 kind of rare.

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:50.420
 We both have a mailbox that's Copyright Creative Commons.

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:53.220
 That's what's cool about this mailbox.

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:55.620
 At some point later, these things start appearing.

00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:58.980
 She goes like, "Whoa, it looks like Chuck is sending me a note."

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:02.420
 But because of his limitation of postcards, he's only sent me one.

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:06.160
 Now I know who it's from and I know who it's to, and I know that's the first of some number

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:07.820
 of messages.

00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:12.220
 And then the next day, out comes another one, because that was the one that sort of went

00:09:12.220 --> 00:09:15.020
 the more southerly route.

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:16.180
 And she goes, "Well, I'm missing something.

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:17.940
 I'll just sit and wait and see what happens."

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:26.460
 And then finally, many, many days later, the one that took the circuitous, most circuitous,

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:30.220
 most roundabout route comes.

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:36.540
 And now Daphne can reassemble the messages, because she has the sequences.

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:38.260
 So she simply puts them back together.

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:41.200
 One, two, three, boom.

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:42.200
 So she puts them back together.

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:43.200
 She has it.

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:47.460
 She sort of throws the postcards away and she has the ultimate final message.

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:49.260
 That's packets.

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:53.540
 Packets are breaking a big message into small parts, labeling each one of them individually,

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:56.940
 and then throwing them into the shared network.

00:09:56.940 --> 00:10:00.460
 And so this is what it looks like.

00:10:00.460 --> 00:10:06.900
 The post office in this is called a gateway.

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:11.300
 Okay, come on.

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:13.460
 Gateway.

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:14.780
 So that's a gateway.

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:18.380
 So here's Michigan, here's Stanford.

00:10:18.380 --> 00:10:19.740
 Stanford has a gateway too.

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:21.560
 That's like their post office.

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:26.740
 In the middle is the post office box.

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:31.780
 And the post office box basically, the post office has intermediate locations and it has

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:34.060
 trucks and locations.

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:36.660
 These are links and routers.

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:47.300
 And messages can take different paths, right?

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:50.120
 And then they arrive and they're reassembled.

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:53.360
 And we have like the ability to hook from home and stuff like that.

00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:56.940
 So there's a local area network, LAN, LAN.

00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:02.100
 And then there's this sort of like internet, the network of networks.

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:03.640
 It's like the post office.

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:05.100
 And it still breaks things into packets.

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:06.660
 They still come out as packets.

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:12.580
 And then Daphne has to reassemble them on her computer to create the message.

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:17.660
 So the research network, ARPANET, solved a lot of engineering problems.

